        "status": "success",
        "text": "Unit tests and integration tests are both essential components of software testing, but they serve different purposes and come with their own tradeoffs. Here\u2019s a comparison of the two:\n\n### Unit Tests\n\n#### Definition:\n- Unit tests are designed to test individual components or functions of the software in isolation from the rest of the system.\n\n#### Tradeoffs:\n\n**Pros:**\n1. **Fast Feedback:** Unit tests are usually quick to run since they test small pieces of code, allowing for rapid feedback during development.\n2. **Isolation:** They focus on a single unit of code, making it easier to pinpoint the source of a failure.\n3. **Easier Debugging:** Failures in unit tests are generally easier to diagnose since they involve less complex interactions.\n4. **Encourages Good Design:** Writing unit tests can encourage developers to write modular, maintainable, and testable code.\n5. **Frequent Execution:** They can be run frequently, such as during development, CI/CD pipelines, and after small changes.\n\n**Cons:**\n1. **Limited Coverage:** Unit tests do not cover interactions between components, which can lead to missing integration issues.\n2. **False Sense of Security:** Passing unit tests do not guarantee that the system works correctly when all units interact.\n3. **Maintenance Overhead:** Unit tests need to be maintained as the code changes, which can lead to increased overhead.\n4. **Mocking Complexity:** They often require mocking dependencies, which can lead to complex setups that might not reflect real-world scenarios.\n\n### Integration Tests\n\n#### Definition:\n- Integration tests evaluate the interactions between multiple components or systems, ensuring they work together as intended.\n\n#### Tradeoffs:\n\n**Pros:**\n1. **Realistic Scenarios:** They simulate real-world usage and interactions between components, providing a better picture of system behavior.\n2. **Identifying Integration Issues:** Integration tests can catch issues related to data flow and communication between components that unit tests might miss.\n3. **End-to-End Testing:** They can cover more extensive workflows, testing the system's functionality from start to finish.\n4. **Confidence in Deployment:** Successful integration tests can increase confidence that the system will behave correctly in production.\n\n**Cons:**\n1. **Slower Execution:** Integration tests are typically slower to run than unit tests, as they involve multiple components and potentially external systems.\n2. **Complex Debugging:** When integration tests fail, it can be more challenging to identify which component is responsible for the failure.\n3. **Higher Setup Cost:** They often require a more",
